\par
\pard {\b B1.} Is my understanding correct that "FPF % of readings \u8805>= 92% of limit when overflowing" measures compliance with the flow-to-treatment condition of the permit \u8212- that is, whether the works was passing forward the required flow at the times the storm overflow was discharging? If not, please explain what it measures.\par
\par
\pard {\b B2.} In 2025 this record shows 336 consecutive days without good Flow Passed Forward data and 350 without good overflow operation data. Please confirm whether that is correct, explain why the monitoring was unavailable for that period, and state when it was restored.\par
\par
\pard {\b B3.} For 2025 the same record shows compliance of 75%, against 100% in each of 2023 and 2024. What does that 25% represent in terms of actual discharges, and was any of it investigated?\par
\par
\pard {\b B4.} For 2025 this record gives 43.1 hours of overflow operation across 19 operations. The Event Duration Monitoring annual return for the same works and the same year (unique ID SVT01571) gives 305.5 hours across 38 counted spills. Please explain the difference between these two published figures, and state which the Agency regards as the record of what actually happened.\par
\par
\pard {\b B5.} During the period when flow monitoring was unavailable, on what basis was compliance with the permit's flow condition assessed?\par
\par
\pard {\b B6.} Please provide a copy of the current permit for this works, including the flow-to-treatment condition and the storm overflow conditions.\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs24 Why I am asking\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard I am aware that the Agency has already reached a conclusion about the effect of these discharges. Your Catchment Data Explorer classifies water body {\b GB104028052511}, "Erewash from Nethergreen Brook to Gilt Brook", as {\b Poor}, and has done since 2016, having been Moderate in 2013, 2014 and 2015.\par
\par
\pard Of the twenty Reasons for Not Achieving Good recorded against that water body, farming and urban run-off are marked Probable. {\b Two are marked Confirmed, and both are sewage}: "Sewage discharge (continuous)" and "Sewage discharge (intermittent)", each attributed to the Water Industry, waste water treatment sector, affecting ammonia.\par
\par
\pard So the Agency has already confirmed that intermittent sewage discharge is a cause of this river's condition. My questions above are about whether the monitoring that governs those discharges was working.\par
\par
\pard {\b B7.} Given that intermittent sewage discharge is recorded as a {\i Confirmed} reason for this water body not achieving good status, what weight was given to the unavailability of flow monitoring at Milnhay works during 2025 when considering compliance and enforcement?\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs28 Part C \u8212- river monitoring in the parish\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard Your Water Quality Archive holds sampling point {\b MD-45217950}, "RIVER EREWASH UPSTREAM OF MILNHAY SEWAGE TREATMENT WORKS". It is the obvious place to measure what the works does to the river, because the next point downstream (MD-45217250, Shipley Gate) is already sampled monthly.\par
\par
\pard {\b C1.} I can find no results at all from MD-45217950 since 2015. Is that correct? If sampling there has ceased, when and why, and is there any plan to reinstate it?\par
\par
\pard {\b C2.} Downstream at Bailey Brook, Milnhay Road (MD-45691150), ammoniacal nitrogen averaged about 0.10 mg/l each year from 2015 to 2021. In 2022 the annual mean was 0.65 mg/l with a peak sample of 3.50 mg/l, and in 2025 the annual mean was 0.81 mg/l with a peak of 2.60 mg/l. Dissolved oxygen at the same point was at its lowest of the record in 2025. Were either of those years investigated, and if so what was found?\par
\par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \par\pard\par
\par
\pard I am not alleging a breach of any kind in any of these matters. I am asking because the published data does not allow me to answer these questions myself, and I think it should.\par
\par
\pard I have set out the working behind all three parts, with every source linked, at https://sewage.beardedbuddy.com/ The River Erewash runs the length of this parish and I would like to understand what is permitted to discharge into it and how that is monitored.\par
\par
\pard I would be grateful for a response within the statutory 20 working days.\par
\par
\pard Yours faithfully,\par
\par
\pard {\b [your name]} [your address] [your email]\par
\par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \par\pard\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs28 What to expect, and what to do with it\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard {\b Part A.} The likeliest answer is a legacy permit for an asset long gone. If so, ask when it will be revoked and how many other dormant sewage permits sit on the register for this catchment \u8212- a live permit for a demolished asset is its own problem. If instead it turns out to be monitored under a reference you could not find, that is a data quality fault in a national dataset, worth reporting and worth telling Top of the Poops about.\par
\par
\pard {\b Part C} is the quiet one, and possibly the most useful. If the upstream point really has recorded nothing since 2015, then nobody \u8212- including the Agency \u8212- can measure what the works does to the river, and that is a straightforward thing to ask them to fix.\par
\par
\pard {\b Part B is the strongest.} B4 is the question to watch: two of the Agency's own published records disagree about the same works in the same year by a factor of seven. There is probably a sound technical reason \u8212- most likely that the 43.1 hours only covers the fraction of the year the instrument was working \u8212- but they should say so, on the record. And if the flow monitoring really was down for 336 days, then for most of 2025 nobody could demonstrate that the works met the condition that makes its spilling lawful.\par
\par
\pard {\b If they miss the deadline}, ask for an internal review, then complain to the Information Commissioner. Both free.\par
\par
\pard {\b Do not run ahead of the answers.} The strength here is that every question is specific, sourced and answerable, and that you have said plainly you are not accusing anyone. That is what makes it hard to fob off.\par
\par
\par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \par\pard\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs34 Follow-up, written after the first letter was sent\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard Two things came to light after that letter went. Neither makes it wrong, but one of them probably supplies the Agency's answer to Part B, and it opens a better question than the one asked.\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs28 What Severn Trent did at Milnhay, which the first letter did not know\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard Between 2022 and 2024 Severn Trent spent around {\b \u163?35.8 million} rebuilding Newthorpe works and {\b closed Milnhay as a treatment works altogether}. Milnhay is now a pumping station: what it collects is pumped 2.5 km to Newthorpe and treated there. Only the storm tanks were kept at Milnhay, their capacity raised from 2 megalitres to 7.\par
\par
\pard {\b Expect that to be the answer to B2 and B4.} If there is no longer any flow passed forward {\i to treatment} at Milnhay, because the treatment moved to Newthorpe, then a flow-to-treatment monitor reporting nothing for 336 days of 2025 has an innocent explanation, and the 43.1 hours on that record against 305.5 in the EDM return is the same story: the instrument covered part of a year in which the site changed use.\par
\par
\pard That is a perfectly good answer and it should be accepted if it comes. It does not touch {\b B5}, which becomes the important question: if the flow-to-treatment condition can no longer be measured at Milnhay, what governs when those storm tanks may lawfully discharge, and how is that assessed?\par
\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b\fs28 The thing worth asking next\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard The permit for Milnhay, {\b T/61/45098/R}, is at {\b version 8, varied on 16 December 2022 and effective from 22 December 2022}. That is before the works closed. On the Agency's public register today it still describes the site as:\par
\par
